# Two Synology NFS mounts: one to read, one to write.
#
# Append to /etc/fstab INSIDE the container, then `mount -a`.
#
# Mounting is deliberately not the application's job — it needs root, and an app
# that mounts filesystems turns every stale handle and credential problem into
# its own bug. fstab does it properly and survives a reboot; the Settings page
# verifies what it finds and explains what to fix.
#
# Replace nas.local with your Synology's address (an IP is more reliable than
# mDNS from inside an LXC) and the export paths with yours. On DSM these are
# under Control Panel → Shared Folder → NFS Permissions.

# 1. DOCUMENTS — read-only.
#
# `ro` is the point. The application never writes here (NFR-7), but a read-only
# mount makes that guarantee enforced by the kernel rather than promised by the
# code. Nothing in a scan, an index, or a correction ever needs to write to
# your documents.
#
# soft,timeo=150,retrans=3: a NAS that goes away must not wedge the scan in
# uninterruptible sleep forever. With `hard` (the default) an unreachable
# server hangs the process unkillably. Soft returns an error instead, the scan
# aborts on its vanish guard, and nothing is deleted.
#
# nfsvers=4.1 unless your DSM is old enough to need 3.
nas.local:/volume1/documents  /mnt/nas/documents  nfs  ro,soft,timeo=150,retrans=3,nfsvers=4.1,noatime,_netdev,nofail  0  0

# 2. BACKUPS — read-write.
#
# Holds the human-authored layer: corrections, notes, entity merges. This is
# the state that cannot be regenerated from your documents, which is what makes
# losing the container survivable.
#
# nofail on both: an unreachable NAS should leave the container booting with a
# working UI, not drop it to an emergency shell. The index is local, so search
# keeps working without the share.
nas.local:/volume1/backups/tracepaper  /mnt/nas/backups/tracepaper  nfs  rw,soft,timeo=150,retrans=3,nfsvers=4.1,noatime,_netdev,nofail  0  0

# The service runs as the `tracepaper` user. Synology NFS squashes uids by
# default: if the backup mount is not writable, either map all users to the
# right uid in DSM's NFS permissions, or set the export's squash to
# "no mapping". The Settings page proves the write rather than trusting the
# permission bits, so it will tell you plainly if this is wrong.
